Carafe: the Switch audio backend counts the buffer and the position a game sees in the game's own frames.
It mixes to 48 kHz and reported its ring in 48 kHz frames as the buffer size and padding, while the game writes
frames at its own rate. Below 48 kHz the first full write did not fit once converted: ReleaseBuffer failed with
AUDCLNT_E_BUFFER_TOO_LARGE, kept the buffer locked, and every later GetBuffer failed with AUDCLNT_E_OUT_OF_ORDER.
Each write was also converted on its own and rounded down, so the position fell a fraction of a frame short per
write; winmm never saw its last header played and the program waited for it forever.
Size, padding and the GetBuffer check now use the game's rate, with one 48 kHz frame kept free for rounding;
padding is rounded up and never reported above the size, which winmm would subtract into a huge request.
Writes are converted from the running total, so no fraction is lost, and the resampler keeps its phase across
writes. The position is the game's frames at the game's rate, never past what was written. A failed
ReleaseBuffer unlocks the buffer, and the conversion is done in 64 bits.
LGPL-2.1-or-later, as the file it changes.
